WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.295 --> 00:00:02.629
A Terrifying Nightmare On the night of April 17, 1906, the city of San Francisco held

2
00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:19.183
one of the most exciting events of its cultural season: the great Italian opera singer Enrico Caruso

3
00:00:19.184 --> 00:00:29.344
was in town to perform Carmen with the Metropolitan Opera Company. After a dazzling performance that

4
00:00:29.345 --> 00:00:34.684
was attended by many of the city's wealthiest and most prominent citizens, Caruso returned to his hotel

5
00:00:34.685 --> 00:00:39.509
room and went to sleep. Early the next morning, his hotel room began rocking like a

6
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:44.010
boat at sea. When Caruso looked out the window, he was met with a terrifying sight.

7
00:00:44.011 --> 00:00:49.240
Buildings were toppling and roads were cracking wide open. The city streets were filled with people running

8
00:00:49.241 --> 00:00:55.929
and screaming in terror. The famous opera singer was visiting San Francisco during the great earthquake

9
00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:04.005
of 1906. One of the worst natural disasters in American history, the earthquake killed thousands of

10
00:01:04.006 --> 00:01:10.847
people and injured many others. More than half of the city's residents became homeless, and over 28,000

11
00:01:10.848 --> 00:01:18.468
buildings were destroyed. As Enrico Caruso later wrote, the morning of April 18, 1906, was a

12
00:01:18.469 --> 00:01:21.563
"dreadful nightmare." Crisis in the City on the Bay In 1906 San Francisco was one of America's

13
00:01:21.564 --> 00:01:31.060
largest and wealthiest cities, with a rapidly rising immigrant population. Located on the San Francisco Bay,

14
00:01:31.061 --> 00:01:40.967
it provided an important gateway to the Pacific Ocean, attracting tourists, artists, and businesspeople. The city

15
00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:50.369
on the bay had experienced earthquakes in the past, but nothing like the one that began at

16
00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:54.371
5:12 a.m. on April 18. The powerful earthquake lasted for nearly a minute. The tremors were

17
00:01:54.372 --> 00:02:04.533
so violent that entire buildings collapsed, trains fell on their sides, and boats in the bay

18
00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:11.085
nearly jumped out of the water. People on the street were crushed by falling stones and debris,

19
00:02:11.086 --> 00:02:15.348
while others were buried in buildings. After the earthquake stopped, the fires began. According to an

20
00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:27.672
account from a mining journal, "Within half an hour . . . a hump of dark smoke

21
00:02:27.673 --> 00:02:36.844
appeared over the city, growing . . . until it rose . . . like the

22
00:02:36.845 --> 00:02:41.741
clouds made from a volcano." Caused by exploding gas lines and overturned stoves and lamps, the

23
00:02:41.742 --> 00:02:47.473
flames swept through the city, burning out of control for days. Unfortunately the earthquake had also ruptured

24
00:02:47.474 --> 00:02:56.233
the city's water lines. It was nearly impossible to obtain water for putting out the raging

25
00:02:56.234 --> 00:03:04.147
fires. Organizing fire-fighting efforts was made more difficult by the fact that the chief of the fire

26
00:03:04.148 --> 00:03:08.461
department had been critically injured during the earthquake. City workers finally began using dynamite to create

27
00:03:08.462 --> 00:03:13.308
spaces between buildings in an attempt to keep fires contained. But most of these men were

28
00:03:13.309 --> 00:03:19.126
not used to handling powerful explosives. This led to more accidents and may have even spread the

29
00:03:19.127 --> 00:03:28.712
fire further. Five hundred blocks of buildings and houses were destroyed by the earthquake and fires.

30
00:03:28.713 --> 00:03:35.456
Hundreds of thousands of city residents, now homeless, flocked to camps in areas such as Oakland,

31
00:03:35.457 --> 00:03:41.702
Berkeley, and Golden Gate Park. The housing crisis was so severe that many of these camps remained

32
00:03:41.703 --> 00:03:49.684
open for up to two years. Other citizens stayed behind in the city. Many went to

33
00:03:49.685 --> 00:03:51.657
Lotta's Fountain each day. The fountain was a city landmark that had become a meeting point for

34
00:03:51.658 --> 00:03:57.186
people desperately searching for missing loved ones. On the Fault Line After the earthquake many people

35
00:03:57.187 --> 00:04:09.434
wondered what had caused such a violent event. It was due to San Francisco's location near

36
00:04:09.435 --> 00:04:16.486
the San Andreas fault, a massive rupture in the earth's crust that stretches some eight hundred miles

37
00:04:16.487 --> 00:04:21.254
from southern to northern California. Faults occur along the places where tectonic plates—rigid sections of the

38
00:04:21.255 --> 00:04:27.989
earth’s crust—meet. As they move, there is a constant strain on the edges of the plates.

39
00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:35.652
Usually the plates' movements are slow and cannot be perceived except by sensitive instruments. But during an

40
00:04:35.653 --> 00:04:37.909
earthquake, the movements are sudden and powerful, as on the morning of the 1906 earthquake. Understanding

41
00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:45.050
the Quake Seismology, the science that deals with earthquakes, was in its early stages in 1906. It

42
00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:52.399
would be another thirty years before the Richter scale was developed, which is used today to

43
00:04:52.400 --> 00:05:01.551
measure the strength of an earthquake. Some experts have estimated that the 1906 earthquake would have

44
00:05:01.552 --> 00:05:09.625
measured around 8.0, which is very high on the Richter scale. The tremors were so great that

45
00:05:09.626 --> 00:05:19.624
they were felt all over southern California as well as in two bordering states. Although it

46
00:05:19.625 --> 00:05:26.123
was later referred to as the San Francisco Earthquake, other cities and towns, such as San

47
00:05:26.124 --> 00:05:34.582
Jose and Santa Rosa, were affected too. In the days following the earthquake, a committee of scientists

48
00:05:34.583 --> 00:05:37.111
began meeting to study its causes. This resulted in the Lawson report of 1908, which is

49
00:05:37.112 --> 00:05:46.021
still considered the most important study of an earthquake ever written. More earthquake studies followed. The most

50
00:05:46.022 --> 00:05:51.824
significant theory was developed by Harry Fielding Reid, who unveiled his elastic rebound theory in 1910.

51
00:05:51.825 --> 00:05:57.903
His theory showed how energy builds up and is then released during an earthquake. Using this

52
00:05:57.904 --> 00:06:08.408
theory, scientists were able to better predict when an earthquake would happen. Reid also proved that earthquakes

53
00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:10.823
are the result of faulting. Today this still serves as the model for understanding earthquake dynamics.

54
00:06:10.824 --> 00:06:18.146
Rising from the Ashes After the earthquake money and food for relief efforts poured into San Francisco

55
00:06:18.147 --> 00:06:24.656
in very large numbers. Within a few days, millions of dollars had been raised by national

56
00:06:24.657 --> 00:06:31.898
and local organizations. The U.S. government also contributed money to set up food kitchens to feed

57
00:06:31.899 --> 00:06:38.310
the massive homeless population. Builders, plumbers, and other workers traveled to the city to help with the

58
00:06:38.311 --> 00:06:43.791
rebuilding effort. Over the next nine years, the destroyed city slowly rose again. The original street

59
00:06:43.792 --> 00:06:52.455
grid was restored, new office buildings and homes were erected, and builders added a new civic

60
00:06:52.456 --> 00:06:59.717
center. People also gained a much better understanding of how buildings could be constructed to withstand earthquakes.

61
00:06:59.718 --> 00:07:04.870
City planners and architects observed which buildings had withstood the earthquake. Soon new regulations were passed

62
00:07:04.871 --> 00:07:11.364
to make buildings better able to tolerate earthquakes. When another serious earthquake occurred in San Francisco on

63
00:07:11.365 --> 00:07:20.837
October 17, 1989, the damage was much less severe. The 1906 earthquake took place the morning

64
00:07:20.838 --> 00:07:25.986
after a great opera performance. In 1910, after years of recovery and rebuilding, another famed singer

65
00:07:25.987 --> 00:07:33.669
came to the city. This time, on a makeshift outdoor stage, the Italian soprano Luisa Tetrazzini sang

66
00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:42.796
for San Francisco. For many of the city's residents, her performance on Christmas Eve was a

67
00:07:42.797 --> 00:07:51.669
joyous occasion that marked the end of the disaster. San Francisco has not forgotten the terrible

68
00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:58.396
tragedy that struck on April 18, 1906. Every year on this date, there is a memorial at

69
00:07:58.397 --> 00:08:08.044
Lotta's Fountain at 5:12 a.m., the exact time when the first tremor was felt. At the

70
00:08:08.045 --> 00:08:16.461
fountain people gather to honor the dead and remember the horror and destruction of the 1906 earthquake.

71
00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:22.120
They also pay tribute to the resilience of the city on the bay and its people.

